WEBVTT

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:51.320
Cumbria.

00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:55.960
The home of much-loved author and illustrator, Beatrix Potter.

00:00:55.040 --> 00:01:04.040
She wasn't just inspired by this sublime setting - as a farmer, she fought to conserve and care for it.

00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:07.400
Which character do you think would have an absolute feast in here?

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:09.040
Peter Rabbit!

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:10.960
Peter Rabbit.

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:18.560
Nearly at the top now.

00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:19.920
This makes it all worth it!

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:26.640
Helen's getting stuck in with the Lakeland games she loved watching as a child.

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:27.480
How do you stay on your feet?

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:29.960
Don't know!

00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:36.120
Tom's investigating the world's most widely used herbicide.

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:42.080
It's used on our farmland, our parks, our gardens, and even our allotments.

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:48.360
So why are there calls from across Europe to ban the use of glyphosate?

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:54.480
And Adam's in Suffolk, where heavy horses are helping to recreate a Capability Brown landscape.

00:01:55.520 --> 00:02:01.440
How do you think we would load this log on here without the aid of any mechanical means?

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:07.800
I don't know how you're going to lift it off the ground.

00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:30.040
The lush Lakeland landscape is nature at her most creative.

00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:39.920
It's captured the imagination of many great artists and writers, who created their own masterpieces here, inspired by these fells.

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:43.760
And one of the most famous of them all is Beatrix Potter.

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:49.840
Born in London, her love affair with the southern Lake District is well documented.

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:59.360
But what many people don't know is that it all began further north, at Lingholm, on the shores of Derwentwater.

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:12.880
It was in these tranquil surroundings as a young woman that she came up with the ideas for her most famous stories - Mrs Tiggy-Winkle, Squirrel Nutkin and Peter Rabbit.

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:24.520
To mark the 150th anniversary of the birth of Beatrix Potter, Jessie Binns, a ranger with the National Trust, has been looking more deeply into the relationship between Potter's works and the landscape.

00:03:23.560 --> 00:03:24.680
Jessie, hello!

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:25.360
Hello!

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:27.040
Very nice to meet you.

00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:34.080
Now, of all the beautiful places in the Lake District, why are we meeting at this particular spot?

00:03:33.160 --> 00:03:36.920
Well, we're in the hamlet of Littletown right here, that she writes about.

00:03:35.960 --> 00:03:43.880
And what we've been finding is that she drew the actual hills that are around here.

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:47.680
She wrote about real people and about real places.

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:47.840
Right.

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:59.920
And she's actually painting the real landscape that's around here, so I thought, well, I wonder if I can track down some of the places where she stood to make those paintings.

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:09.880
Luckily, the rangers who work with me, some of them have worked in these valleys all their lives, so they said, "OK, we'll start in this area, start in that area."

00:04:08.920 --> 00:04:15.600
And then once you start looking, suddenly you kind of come round a corner and go, "That's it!

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:16.440
"That's it!"

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:20.959
So you've got all these big burly rangers reading Mrs Tiggy-Winkle!

00:04:20.079 --> 00:04:21.640
Absolutely!

00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:22.360
Yeah.

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:24.440
Trying to find the actual places.

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:34.080
Suddenly, we've stepped right into the painting of Lucy, from Mrs Tiggy-Winkle.

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:39.960
You can see, can't you, there, the top of the crag coming down and that beautiful rounded hilltop in the background?

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:41.640
She's captured it beautifully, hasn't she?

00:04:40.920 --> 00:04:42.040
Yeah.

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:48.720
Have you said to the people in the farmhouse, "Do you realise that your house is in a Beatrix Potter book?"

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:50.400
No, I've been too scared!

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:52.360
Well, maybe they're watching Countryfile now.

00:04:51.480 --> 00:04:54.800
If you are, congratulations!

00:04:55.600 --> 00:05:01.560
"Lucy scrambled up the hill as fast "as her stout legs would carry her.

00:05:00.760 --> 00:05:06.560
"She ran along a steep pathway, up and up, "until Littletown was right away down below."

00:05:10.520 --> 00:05:18.480
Another famous tale that comes straight out of the landscape at Lingholm is Squirrel Nutkin.

00:05:17.720 --> 00:05:20.480
So this is quite a famous tree, this one, Jessie.

00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:24.640
Yeah, and when you look at this you can really see why.

00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:25.320
I mean, that is absolutely...

00:05:24.840 --> 00:05:26.440
Oh, my word!

00:05:25.640 --> 00:05:27.200
Isn't it?

00:05:26.360 --> 00:05:27.880
Yes, it is!

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:29.680
Isn't that great?

00:05:28.760 --> 00:05:33.000
It's bang on!

00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:40.160
This is from The Tale of Squirrel Nutkin, and it's the squirrels rafting to the island.

00:05:41.160 --> 00:05:43.560
It's such a beautiful...

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:44.200
Great idea.

00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:44.560
Isn't it great?

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:54.680
And I've been going round and round Derwentwater looking at this island from all angles, trying to make the hills in the background match up, and I think this is closest I've seen.

00:05:54.520 --> 00:06:03.960
But also we've got this fantastic clue, because this is one of the very few photographs of Beatrix actually sketching in the landscape.

00:06:03.200 --> 00:06:13.040
We're pretty sure that this photograph was taken on St Herbert's Island looking that way.

00:06:12.200 --> 00:06:13.640
So we know that she visited the island.

00:06:12.680 --> 00:06:16.240
We've got proof that she was there.

00:06:15.520 --> 00:06:18.720
What you hope to do with all of this evidence that you've now accumulated?

00:06:17.840 --> 00:06:19.560
Ah!

00:06:18.600 --> 00:06:36.360
Well, my grand plan, if I can make it work, is I'd love to actually install replicas of her original watercolours in the landscape temporarily, so that people can physically stand at the spot where Beatrix stood and feel as inspired by it as she was.

00:06:35.400 --> 00:06:43.400
And then see the artwork that she produced from that.

00:06:43.480 --> 00:06:50.040
And I think if we can make that connection between her love of the lakes and the landscape around it, I think it would be an amazing thing to be able to share with people.

00:06:50.600 --> 00:07:00.160
"They made little rafts out of twigs, "and they paddled away "over the water to Owl Island "to gather nuts."

00:07:01.200 --> 00:07:15.600
It certainly feels magical to be standing on the spot where Beatrix Potter dreamt up some of her wonderful wild heroes.

00:07:14.880 --> 00:07:29.120
Now, it's the world's most used herbicide, so why is it that across the UK and Europe there's an argument to ban glyphosate?

00:07:28.280 --> 00:07:33.120
Here's Tom.

00:07:32.600 --> 00:07:45.760
Today's farmers have many tools at their disposal, but when it comes to using the world's bestselling weedkiller, glyphosate, it isn't without controversy.

00:07:44.880 --> 00:07:56.080
Around three-quarters of a million tonnes of this staff, glyphosate, are used on our farmland across the world every year.

00:07:55.280 --> 00:08:00.000
But now some people say it's unsafe and could increase the risk of cancer.

00:07:59.200 --> 00:08:03.760
And the European Union is considering a ban.

00:08:11.640 --> 00:08:17.320
So when you're spraying this, are you driving yourself or you get a bit of satellite assistance?

00:08:16.520 --> 00:08:23.720
Satellite assistance guiding the steering of it, the direction of it and also for switching the chemical on and off.

00:08:24.280 --> 00:08:32.880
Andrew Ward farms 1,600 acres of arable land in Lincolnshire.

00:08:32.080 --> 00:08:43.760
Today, he isn't spraying glyphosate, but when he does, he uses it to wage chemical warfare on one of the most prolific weeds that farmers face - blackgrass.

00:08:42.880 --> 00:08:44.919
Give me a feeling of the timetable of how you'd use it in fields like this.

00:08:44.280 --> 00:08:45.400
The field we're in at the minute is sugar beet.

00:08:44.440 --> 00:08:55.480
It was sprayed on the bare soil, as the blackgrass is germinated in the autumn, and then it was sprayed again in the spring before we sow the crop.

00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:56.360
So that would be two applications.

00:08:57.360 --> 00:08:58.480
And in a wheat field, maybe, like...

00:08:57.800 --> 00:09:03.240
In a wheat field like that, again it depends how soon the field is cultivated after harvest.

00:09:02.560 --> 00:09:05.040
And so our aim is to get as many glyphosates on as we can.

00:09:04.320 --> 00:09:11.400
So in most fields, they'd often be getting two or maybe three goes with glyphosate?

00:09:12.120 --> 00:09:15.360
They would with us, yes, but a lot of farmers, probably only one.

00:09:14.520 --> 00:09:25.160
Glyphosate is the only effective weedkiller on the market that can rid a field of blackgrass.

00:09:24.320 --> 00:09:26.080
But that's not the only way it's being used on farms.

00:09:25.240 --> 00:09:28.360
It can also be used to dry wheat before harvest.

00:09:27.560 --> 00:09:34.760
In a wet year, like we're having at the moment, the wheat is slow to mature and it ripens very unevenly.

00:09:34.880 --> 00:09:45.360
Farmers use it to ripen their crops so that the millers then have a better availability of premium red wheat so they can actually make better quality loaves of bread.

00:09:44.520 --> 00:09:55.360
For farmers like Andrew, glyphosate is more than just a useful tool - it's an essential part of agriculture that he says he can't do without.

00:09:54.400 --> 00:09:57.360
And it's not just farming that relies on this weedkiller.

00:09:56.760 --> 00:10:08.920
The next time you sit on a park bench, lean on a lamppost or pass a roadside tree, there's a good chance that glyphosate will have been sprayed around them.

00:10:08.040 --> 00:10:23.360
Introduced in the 1970s by the biotech giant Monsanto, today, glyphosate is widely used to keep railway lines free of weeds and by councils in public places.

00:10:22.400 --> 00:10:35.400
And you might even find it in your garden shed or on the allotment, because glyphosate is the active ingredient in the world's bestselling weedkiller, Roundup.

00:10:34.600 --> 00:10:43.520
But despite its wide-spread use, there are growing calls for it to be banned due to safety concerns.

00:10:43.800 --> 00:10:52.840
We've got to get a little bit more relaxed about having a few more weeds, plants out of place in our farmed environment.

00:10:52.040 --> 00:11:01.720
Helen Browning runs a 1,400-acre organic farm in Wiltshire, and is the chief executive of the Soil Association.

00:11:00.800 --> 00:11:09.240
We've got a fairly typical picnic here in front of us - a loaf of bread, sandwiches and a pasty, but the news about glyphosate gives you some concerns about this.

00:11:08.400 --> 00:11:11.120
Tell me.

00:11:10.480 --> 00:11:13.000
Well, glyphosate has been cited as a probable carcinogen.

00:11:12.120 --> 00:11:25.920
Surveys have shown that something like two-thirds of bread products have glyphosate residues in them, it's turning up in breast milk, in our urine, so this chemical is becoming ubiquitous.

00:11:25.080 --> 00:11:26.320
It's getting into us on a regular basis.

00:11:25.400 --> 00:11:32.960
Now that there is this concern over its carcinogenic properties, we've got to stop that.

00:11:32.080 --> 00:11:45.720
Last year, the World Health Organisation listed glyphosate as a probable human carcinogen or, in other words, it probably increases the risk of cancer.

00:11:44.960 --> 00:11:59.160
For such a widely used herbicide to be listed as probably carcinogenic sounds fairly scary, but it's worth remembering what's in the same category - being a hairdresser, for instance.

00:11:58.280 --> 00:12:07.760
And of greater risk is sunlight - little of it today, granted - and also alcohol, listed as carcinogenic.

00:12:06.920 --> 00:12:20.520
It's these safety concerns that led campaign groups like the Soil Association to call for a ban on the use of glyphosate on crops just before they're harvested and from being used in public spaces.

00:12:19.880 --> 00:12:21.640
Their demands have been gathering momentum.

00:12:23.920 --> 00:12:40.840
Recently, glyphosate came close to being banned across Europe, but was given an 18-month stay of execution by the European Commission while they consider a new report into its safety.

00:12:39.920 --> 00:12:49.600
Even though our nation recently voted for Brexit, UK farmers could still be affected by a European ban.

00:12:48.720 --> 00:12:58.920
That's because even when we officially leave the EU, farmers could be stopped from exporting foods containing traces of glyphosate onto the continent.

00:12:57.960 --> 00:13:04.240
If there had been a decision in Europe a few weeks ago to ban glyphosate, what would that have meant to your farm?

00:13:03.360 --> 00:13:10.680
It would have really been catastrophic for the farm, because we'd have had to grass down big areas and then cease growing things on there.

00:13:09.880 --> 00:13:15.080
It would then question whether it was actually worthwhile carrying on farming in the other part of the farm.

00:13:14.400 --> 00:13:20.880
So it's not an exaggeration to say if you couldn't use glyphosate it might question your future in farming?

00:13:20.080 --> 00:13:25.360
I don't think it is an exaggeration at all.

00:13:24.600 --> 00:13:30.720
The National Farmers Union agree with Andrew that a complete ban would be very costly.

00:13:29.920 --> 00:13:40.120
They estimate that more than £500 million-worth of production would be lost each year without the use of glyphosate.

00:13:39.280 --> 00:13:46.000
But does it really pose as serious a risk to health as being claimed?

00:13:45.040 --> 00:14:02.840
Many of those who are convinced glyphosate is safe say opposition to it is driven by a desire to cripple its leading manufacturer - a company seen by some as the bogeyman of modern farming, Monsanto.

00:14:02.000 --> 00:14:05.120
So, is that true, and can we farm without it?

00:14:04.280 --> 00:14:06.680
I'll be finding out later.

00:14:08.480 --> 00:14:17.600
The beginning of the 20th century saw the arrival of tractors powered by petrol and diesel.

00:14:16.640 --> 00:14:22.360
Before then, our farmland was shaped and cultivated by horse and steam power.

00:14:21.440 --> 00:14:33.000
You may think that as soon as mechanisation came along, working horses became redundant, but actually there was a time at the turn of the century when old and new worked side by side.

00:14:32.200 --> 00:14:39.360
Here at Old Hall Farm in Cumbria, they still do.

00:14:38.480 --> 00:14:44.400
Husband and wife Alex and Charlotte Sharphouse are combining their two passions.

00:14:43.520 --> 00:14:51.960
Charlotte loves working with heavy horses, whilst Alex prefers something a bit more up-to-date.

00:14:52.880 --> 00:14:56.320
Charlotte!

00:14:55.440 --> 00:14:56.720
Who is this fella?

00:14:55.760 --> 00:14:57.720
This is Troy.

00:14:56.760 --> 00:15:00.280
Now, talk me through to how you got to this point in your life.

00:14:59.600 --> 00:15:05.800
Ten years ago, we bought this derelict farm, and we set about farming a traditional Lakeland farm.

00:15:04.920 --> 00:15:12.800
It's all about the forgotten skills, the forgotten arts, the forgotten machinery.

00:15:11.960 --> 00:15:13.400
So it's still a working farm?

00:15:12.560 --> 00:15:15.640
It's still a working farm.

00:15:14.800 --> 00:15:17.200
We farm it traditionally.

00:15:16.360 --> 00:15:19.720
We've just got about 120 acres.

00:15:18.960 --> 00:15:23.720
So a traditional farm would have done a bit of dairy, a bit of arable, a bit of beef.

00:15:22.760 --> 00:15:24.360
So that kind of bucks the trend.

00:15:23.520 --> 00:15:24.840
Most...

00:15:23.960 --> 00:15:31.480
There are a lot of people leaving farming, yet you've spent a decade investing in it and trying to set up a farm.

00:15:30.600 --> 00:15:32.000
Absolutely.

00:15:31.240 --> 00:15:34.200
We certainly are bucking the trend.

00:15:33.440 --> 00:15:39.520
When you work with the horses, you can think, "Yeah, that's why tractors came!"

00:15:38.760 --> 00:15:44.840
Troy's raring to go and I'm also being put to work.

00:15:44.320 --> 00:15:48.040
It's time to harvest some potatoes.

00:15:48.520 --> 00:15:50.560
Come on, Troy.

00:15:49.600 --> 00:15:50.760
Come on, lad.

00:15:50.040 --> 00:15:51.360
Back up.

00:15:50.640 --> 00:15:53.120
Reins next.

00:15:52.240 --> 00:15:53.520
Just want to go up through the ring.

00:15:52.680 --> 00:15:54.200
OK, Troy.

00:15:54.520 --> 00:15:57.280
Walk on.

00:15:56.360 --> 00:15:57.880
We've got this, Troy.

00:15:57.280 --> 00:15:58.480
We're away.

00:15:57.560 --> 00:15:59.000
Teamwork now.

00:15:58.040 --> 00:15:59.200
Walk on.

00:15:58.480 --> 00:16:00.000
So I just have to...?

00:15:59.040 --> 00:16:01.440
Steer where the potatoes are.

00:16:00.600 --> 00:16:01.760
Aim down the middle.

00:16:01.640 --> 00:16:04.280
Ah!

00:16:03.320 --> 00:16:04.560
I've plunged it off track, haven't I?

00:16:04.560 --> 00:16:10.040
I'm really sorry, but I've missed the line, haven't I?

00:16:09.200 --> 00:16:13.040
That's my profit gone.

00:16:12.160 --> 00:16:13.560
Walk on, Troy.

00:16:12.880 --> 00:16:14.040
Walk on.

00:16:13.080 --> 00:16:14.200
Good boy.

00:16:14.800 --> 00:16:24.640
Towards the end of the 19th century, horses were being replaced, and this was a more familiar scene on farms across the land.

00:16:23.880 --> 00:16:27.840
This is where Alex comes into his own.

00:16:27.120 --> 00:16:31.480
These are unbelievable!

00:16:31.400 --> 00:16:33.360
So what do you do with these?

00:16:32.920 --> 00:16:35.440
These are a pair of ploughing engines.

00:16:34.880 --> 00:16:38.000
You can see the two massive winch drums underneath the engines.

00:16:37.160 --> 00:16:44.840
You park the engine each side of the field and pull the implement between them.

00:16:44.120 --> 00:16:47.440
It was the very first form of mechanisation, after the horse, with steam.

00:16:46.680 --> 00:16:52.120
It revolutionised, really, land cultivation on a decent scale.

00:16:51.360 --> 00:16:54.560
Yet these didn't replace horses overnight, did they?

00:16:53.680 --> 00:16:55.800
No, certainly not.

00:16:55.000 --> 00:17:01.000
These particular engines are 1920, but you still needed a horse to be able to fetch water to them.

00:17:00.160 --> 00:17:07.200
They'd use 1,000 gallons of water between them in a working day, a tonne of coal each.

00:17:06.319 --> 00:17:15.599
Then, because of the size of them, they could obviously only do the big lumps of land, so you'd still need the horse to finish off and then tidy up afterwards.

00:17:15.040 --> 00:17:18.040
I know you're a pretty resourceful man, but there's resourceful and then there's off-the-scale resourceful.

00:17:17.200 --> 00:17:19.160
I'm looking around here...

00:17:18.359 --> 00:17:23.800
How many bits and bobs have you built and created and fixed up?

00:17:23.319 --> 00:17:24.480
Everything we have, really.

00:17:23.599 --> 00:17:28.480
I don't buy anything that's done, really.

00:17:27.680 --> 00:17:33.720
A year ago, Alex and his team took on their most ambitious project yet.

00:17:32.760 --> 00:17:36.480
Talisman.

00:17:35.640 --> 00:17:42.080
It's considered to be the king of the steam world.

00:17:42.240 --> 00:17:49.960
At the turn of the 20th century, it was the ultimate vehicle for heavy haulage and farm work.

00:17:49.160 --> 00:17:58.680
With only one left in existence, Alex is attempting the formidable task of making his very own Talisman from scratch.

00:17:58.400 --> 00:18:00.240
This is a serious-looking piece of kit.

00:17:59.920 --> 00:18:05.680
This is the front, the smoke box, where the wheels fit underneath on the axle.

00:18:04.800 --> 00:18:09.840
Why do you think it's important to have something like this right here in the 21st century?

00:18:08.920 --> 00:18:17.640
I'm a traditionalist, and I want to show people what the old skills were and how good they were.

00:18:16.840 --> 00:18:25.080
A lot of the things we're doing on this now have been forgotten and can't be done, so we're having to reinvent the wheel a little bit.

00:18:25.200 --> 00:18:28.720
Well, it's never going to get finished if I stand here gabbing and asking questions, so can I help?

00:18:27.760 --> 00:18:28.960
Yeah, you certainly can.

00:18:28.080 --> 00:18:31.200
We're going to have a go at putting some rivets in.

00:18:30.520 --> 00:18:40.200
'Alex has got to a pivotal point in his build - 'Talisman is about to take shape.

00:18:39.360 --> 00:18:41.240
'Now for my part in the process.

00:18:40.360 --> 00:18:42.320
I hope I don't mess it up.'

00:18:41.480 --> 00:18:42.920
Don't drop it.

00:18:42.000 --> 00:18:44.000
As fast as you can.

00:18:43.040 --> 00:18:44.120
Don't...

00:18:43.240 --> 00:18:45.480
Don't hurry me.

00:18:49.600 --> 00:18:54.520
Oh, when you said, "Hurry up", I was like, "Why are you rushing me?

00:18:54.120 --> 00:18:56.800
"This is a tricky business."

00:18:56.000 --> 00:18:58.280
But you need it to be hot, right?

00:18:59.520 --> 00:19:03.040
It's got to be hot.

00:19:06.040 --> 00:19:07.400
Just put it down now.

00:19:10.240 --> 00:19:13.480
Impressive.

00:19:12.600 --> 00:19:13.720
Well done.

00:19:12.840 --> 00:19:16.360
Look how happy you are!

00:19:15.600 --> 00:19:17.200
'What an incredible achievement.

00:19:16.480 --> 00:19:21.080
'Now there's only one thing left to do.'

00:19:27.120 --> 00:19:40.200
'With Talisman well under way, I want to see what it feels like 'to be behind the wheel of one of these remarkable machines.'

00:19:50.160 --> 00:20:07.000
You know, for most people, steam power and hoof power are outdated, but one thing you cannot argue with is that this farm is powered by passion.

00:20:22.400 --> 00:20:38.120
Beatrix Potter explored the length and breadth of the Lake District, taking the world she saw around her and reinventing it in delicate watercolours.

00:20:37.600 --> 00:20:45.360
Her stories may be quaint and gentle renditions of natural history, but Beatrix Potter herself was anything but a wilting violet.

00:20:44.520 --> 00:20:53.600
She was deeply involved in farming, and her impassioned campaigning certainly ruffled a few feathers.

00:20:52.880 --> 00:21:05.800
I have always thought it somewhat odd that the lady, who has a perfectly competent husband, should insist on managing every detail of farms and woodland problems herself.

00:21:12.920 --> 00:21:26.240
Inspired by Potter's feistier side, local artist Freya Pocklington has set out to paint Cumbria's colourful countrywomen.

00:21:25.520 --> 00:21:28.400
Hi, Freya.

00:21:27.560 --> 00:21:28.800
Hello.

00:21:27.880 --> 00:21:30.600
Whoa, your paintings are amazing.

00:21:29.880 --> 00:21:32.200
Thank you.

00:21:31.360 --> 00:21:33.160
So much going on in them, isn't there?

00:21:32.280 --> 00:21:35.880
Really colourful and in-your-face.

00:21:34.920 --> 00:21:38.440
Where did the whole idea come from?

00:21:37.800 --> 00:21:46.160
I like looking at just different animals and their quirkiness, which Beatrix Potter did as well.

00:21:45.360 --> 00:21:58.040
But I wanted to look at more contemporary issues as well, and show what she did in a new light, and promote her farming as well as the fact that she was an illustrator and artist.

00:21:57.360 --> 00:22:02.000
I thought the farming side of what she did was really admirable and amazing.

00:22:01.480 --> 00:22:17.560
Freya is an artist in residence at the National Trust's Acorn Bank, and some of her subjects might look familiar to regular Countryfile viewers, like the Wool Clip members Matt met last year.

00:22:18.000 --> 00:22:21.200
I like the fact that they were women working together.

00:22:20.400 --> 00:22:26.200
They started working after foot and mouth, realising that they had to approach the wool industry differently.

00:22:25.360 --> 00:22:30.840
And I like the fact that they were businesswomen, being successful at what they did as well.

00:22:30.880 --> 00:22:37.560
We've also met Sarah Lunn on the programme, a busy, rural vicar with livestock of her own.

00:22:36.720 --> 00:22:41.480
I was really fascinated that she's a vicar, but she has all these different types of animals.

00:22:40.720 --> 00:22:43.720
She has a very, very busy life.

00:22:42.840 --> 00:22:51.600
She's looking after 12 different areas, and I think when she goes back to her animals, that's where she has her own time.

00:22:51.520 --> 00:23:03.920
Freya's current painting features horse whisperer Victoria Smith, who started working with animals whilst recovering from a serious illness.

00:23:03.040 --> 00:23:04.360
I was fascinated with Victoria.

00:23:03.400 --> 00:23:08.000
I thought she was such a strong, powerful, lovely lady and very humble with it.

00:23:07.760 --> 00:23:10.680
Such a fascinating story about her as well.

00:23:11.040 --> 00:23:12.960
So is this how you work?

00:23:12.000 --> 00:23:13.680
You start with an outline and then you fill in the detail?

00:23:12.720 --> 00:23:13.840
Yes.

00:23:12.960 --> 00:23:20.520
Yeah, I start using something like this Conte pastel here, and I just do lots of different marks with it, like this.

00:23:19.640 --> 00:23:24.680
Then, after that, I get some tissue and I rub it in.

00:23:24.160 --> 00:23:27.760
It kind of creates movement around the picture.

00:23:28.480 --> 00:23:34.080
Often what I do is I would put a layer of ink.

00:23:33.120 --> 00:23:37.200
So it's a little bit like oil painting, where you're building it up.

00:23:36.280 --> 00:23:42.000
So I put the pastel on, then I put the ink, then I put the pastel again and then the ink.

00:23:41.240 --> 00:23:44.840
So it's quite painterly, even though it's drawing.

00:23:43.880 --> 00:23:45.240
Just let it drip?

00:23:44.280 --> 00:23:46.760
Yeah, just let it drip.

00:23:56.560 --> 00:24:02.200
What do you think Beatrix Potter would think of all the characters in your pictures?

00:24:01.480 --> 00:24:05.360
I think she'd be fascinated in the women.

00:24:04.560 --> 00:24:08.720
I think they are people that she would admire too.

00:24:07.920 --> 00:24:12.440
I think they're very strong but humble women.

00:24:11.680 --> 00:24:17.800
And I think that they are making a massive contribution to the landscape here in the northwest.

00:24:17.360 --> 00:24:19.960
Yeah, they're just fantastic women.

00:24:20.840 --> 00:24:30.320
One of Freya's subjects who has a lot in common with Beatrix Potter is local farmer Susan Aglionby.

00:24:29.400 --> 00:24:33.360
Hello, Jeremiah.

00:24:32.520 --> 00:24:33.760
How are you, darling?

00:24:32.960 --> 00:24:35.120
Are you a good boy?

00:24:35.040 --> 00:24:43.280
Freya has invited me to come and meet her and her companion in the painting - longhorn bull Jeremiah.

00:24:43.280 --> 00:24:46.040
Just give him a stroke there, all right?

00:24:45.120 --> 00:24:46.360
Hi, Jeremiah.

00:24:46.800 --> 00:24:47.960
Whoa.

00:24:47.120 --> 00:24:51.640
Susan, I understand your family ties here in Cumbria go back a long way.

00:24:51.880 --> 00:24:57.000
Almost 900 years.

00:24:56.200 --> 00:25:00.320
My husband's family lived round here.

00:24:59.480 --> 00:25:11.280
When we could escape from London, very similarly to Beatrix Potter, I thought, "Gosh, if she can start a new career at 45 and farm, perhaps I could."

00:25:10.320 --> 00:25:13.560
So she really was an inspiration to me.

00:25:12.640 --> 00:25:19.160
And like Beatrix Potter, you have a real interest in preserving the countryside, especially farms.

00:25:18.680 --> 00:25:19.840
Yes.

00:25:18.960 --> 00:25:21.840
I want people to appreciate where their food comes from.

00:25:21.160 --> 00:25:28.880
Healthy soil producing healthy plants, which produces healthy animals, which produces healthy children.

00:25:28.080 --> 00:25:30.760
It's just wonderful.

00:25:29.880 --> 00:25:33.360
You are a real-life Beatrix Potter, aren't you?

00:25:32.600 --> 00:25:33.760
No, no.

00:25:32.880 --> 00:25:34.320
I'm not an artist!

00:25:33.440 --> 00:25:36.280
I'm not an artist, sadly.

00:25:35.480 --> 00:25:39.000
But Freya is, and she's painted you.

00:25:38.960 --> 00:25:40.120
What do you think of the picture?

00:25:39.320 --> 00:25:41.480
I think it's very striking.

00:25:40.760 --> 00:25:43.760
It's very, very strong.

00:25:42.920 --> 00:25:44.280
It looks really wintry.

00:25:43.440 --> 00:25:49.720
I mean, that skill of being able to get the coldness of winter, which it was...

00:25:48.960 --> 00:25:52.920
It was a dreich day that she came to see us.

00:25:52.120 --> 00:25:54.720
It was, yes, it really was.

00:25:58.000 --> 00:26:04.240
Freya's final paintings will be on display in August.

00:26:03.320 --> 00:26:11.800
A tribute to a different side of Beatrix Potter and the other strong women who nurture and work in this beautiful Lakeland landscape.

00:26:15.680 --> 00:26:27.560
Now, earlier we heard about the controversy surrounding the herbicide glyphosate, used widely by farmers, councils and gardeners.

00:26:26.920 --> 00:26:34.640
However, there are some who say that the health risks are exaggerated, as Tom's been finding out.

00:26:41.600 --> 00:26:52.080
For many farmers, the herbicide glyphosate is an essential part of agriculture.

00:26:51.360 --> 00:27:00.400
But, as I've been hearing, there's a gladiatorial clash under way - a battle over how it can be used in the future.

00:26:59.520 --> 00:27:01.160
Its opponents say it increases the risk of cancer.

00:27:00.400 --> 00:27:07.000
So, how do those who want to keep farming with glyphosate respond to these health claims?

00:27:07.360 --> 00:27:12.680
When you get wet weather at the flowering of the wheat, you tend to get this business.

00:27:11.840 --> 00:27:14.240
This is fusarium.

00:27:13.360 --> 00:27:16.240
That's a fungal disease on there.

00:27:15.440 --> 00:27:17.480
It is, yeah.

00:27:16.680 --> 00:27:23.640
Sean Sparling is vice chairman of the Association of Independent Crop Consultants.

00:27:23.000 --> 00:27:25.680
He believes that any health risks have been exaggerated.

00:27:24.960 --> 00:27:36.080
Is it right that we're spreading this chemical the World Health Organisation says is probably carcinogenic over 2 million hectares?

00:27:35.120 --> 00:27:38.760
That's an area the size of Wales.

00:27:37.920 --> 00:27:39.480
We're dousing the country in a carcinogen.

00:27:38.920 --> 00:27:52.760
The majority, the vast majority of experts within the European Food Standards Authority have said it is not carcinogenic and probably would not cause any damage to human health over the course of a lifetime.

00:27:51.880 --> 00:28:08.240
As we stand at the moment, if you work the figures out, you'd have to eat somewhere between 750 and 1,000 loaves of bread every day for a number of years in order to get anything close to the maximum residue level, which is actually about a hundredth of the top line, if you like, for a dangerous dose.

00:28:08.440 --> 00:28:16.400
Given that you are convinced that glyphosate is safe, why do you think it is that there's such a powerful lobby to get rid of it?

00:28:15.560 --> 00:28:19.440
One word - Monsanto.

00:28:18.480 --> 00:28:30.360
There are groups of people around the world who feel that if we stop Monsanto producing glyphosate, we stop GM crops in the future.

00:28:29.520 --> 00:28:34.480
The problem with that argument is it's not just Monsanto that makes glyphosate.

00:28:33.600 --> 00:28:42.200
So Monsanto and one of their most successful products, glyphosate, has become a symbol for those who dislike the way modern farming has gone.

00:28:41.480 --> 00:28:42.600
Absolutely.

00:28:41.800 --> 00:28:42.920
That is it in a nutshell.

00:28:42.040 --> 00:28:55.280
The damage that will do to UK agriculture is almost incalculable, not just UK but world agriculture.

00:28:55.760 --> 00:29:08.840
Monsanto's role in agriculture, including being among the first to develop GM products, has long made the company controversial.

00:29:08.840 --> 00:29:15.280
Worldwide, they sell up to £3.7 billion-worth of glyphosate-based herbicides each year.

00:29:14.760 --> 00:29:20.440
But you won't find any being sprayed on this organic farm in Wiltshire.

00:29:19.560 --> 00:29:25.160
This is a crop of spelt, which is a sort of old-fashioned version of wheat.

00:29:24.320 --> 00:29:31.320
It's becoming increasingly popular for people who are slightly worried about their gluten intake, because it has a lower level of gluten.

00:29:30.360 --> 00:29:40.040
So does Helen Browning from the Soil Association agree there's a green conspiracy against Monsanto?

00:29:39.080 --> 00:29:45.920
Isn't it the truth that a lot of opposition to glyphosate is driven by a hatred of its creator, the company Monsanto?

00:29:45.280 --> 00:29:48.320
I don't think that's driving this at all.

00:29:47.560 --> 00:30:00.320
I think there are concerns about the corporate control of agriculture, the way that farmers are being constantly sold products and just how much of all of that technology is wrapped up in a few very big companies.

00:29:59.760 --> 00:30:05.760
I can really understand why farmers feel so nervous about losing glyphosate.

00:30:04.880 --> 00:30:13.480
It's been a key tool for them for the last 40 years, so it's going to mean that we have to be a bit more inventive about how we farm.

00:30:12.640 --> 00:30:16.400
I think that could be good for our countryside.

00:30:15.480 --> 00:30:19.000
So what do you think is the alternative to using glyphosate?

00:30:18.200 --> 00:30:25.480
Obviously, on organic farms we've never used this product, so a crop like this will be naturally harvested, it will ripen naturally.

00:30:24.680 --> 00:30:31.840
We use a lot of other techniques to help control weeds, like undersowing.

00:30:30.960 --> 00:30:36.720
There's lots of techniques that organic farmers are using that we can share with non-organic farmers too.

00:30:35.960 --> 00:30:49.800
Perhaps it's no surprise that Helen champions taking a more organic approach, but does stopping using glyphosate to dry wheat before harvest simply mean higher costs for us all?

00:30:49.200 --> 00:31:07.960
If we're taking the issue of glyphosate use on wheat pre-harvest, that might incur a slightly additional cost for farmers, I think most members of the public would feel they'd rather pay an extra half a penny for their loaf of bread and know that it hasn't got that dangerous chemical in it.

00:31:07.120 --> 00:31:15.320
It will, of course, be down to the appetite of the public to stomach any increased costs, however much they might add up to.

00:31:15.200 --> 00:31:27.440
But, for now, the use of glyphosate is allowed, with strings attached.

00:31:26.600 --> 00:31:29.200
The decision on its future is expected within the next 18 months.

00:31:28.720 --> 00:31:40.520
The European Commission is waiting on further public consultation, and a scientific review, but it seems unlikely that this will deliver a decisive verdict, a knockout blow for either side.

00:31:39.680 --> 00:31:50.120
In the end, this will be a political decision, with the way we grow and farm at stake.

00:31:52.920 --> 00:32:06.120
I'm in Cumbria, discovering the dramatic landscape which inspired Beatrix Potter to write some of her most famous stories.

00:32:05.280 --> 00:32:17.000
Beatrix Potter loved the Lake District, and for ten years, her family would spend their summers here at Lingholm on the banks of Derwentwater.

00:32:16.080 --> 00:32:27.560
And it was here that she got the idea for her most celebrated creation, the one who would begin it all - a misbehaving rabbit called Peter.

00:32:27.560 --> 00:32:31.920
"First he ate some lettuces and some French beans.

00:32:31.160 --> 00:32:33.480
"Then he ate some radishes.

00:32:32.640 --> 00:32:38.000
"Then, feeling rather sick, he went to look for some parsley."

00:32:37.440 --> 00:32:48.280
Potter herself said that the kitchen garden here was the basis for Mr McGregor's garden in the story.

00:32:47.400 --> 00:32:52.400
The new owners are completely restoring it so that it can be open to the public.

00:32:51.520 --> 00:33:02.120
Lingholm has its own version of Mr McGregor too, but unlike the fictitious gardener, who chased wildlife, Ken Swift nurtures it.

00:33:01.240 --> 00:33:13.440
The woods here on the estate were the inspiration for Potter's story of Squirrel Nutkin, and Ken is keen to boost the numbers of red squirrels living here.

00:33:12.640 --> 00:33:15.800
So this is our red squirrel feeder.

00:33:14.960 --> 00:33:18.600
We're trying to get the red squirrel population back up.

00:33:17.640 --> 00:33:21.000
By feeding, it allows us to monitor the amount of reds that we've got.

00:33:20.640 --> 00:33:22.960
How often do you see them?

00:33:22.200 --> 00:33:24.920
Once or twice a week.

00:33:24.160 --> 00:33:27.040
You tend to get them scurrying up and down trees.

00:33:26.200 --> 00:33:29.520
You hear them chittering in the tree tops at you sometimes.

00:33:28.680 --> 00:33:30.040
It's lovely.

00:33:31.200 --> 00:33:32.280
Let's have a look at the food, then.

00:33:31.400 --> 00:33:32.920
We've got all sorts of wonderful things in here, then.

00:33:31.960 --> 00:33:37.440
Are you feeding them anything specific to try and attract them?

00:33:36.760 --> 00:33:41.000
It's just a general mix, really - peanuts and seeds.

00:33:40.040 --> 00:33:43.680
There's some sweetcorn and bits and bobs like that.

00:33:43.320 --> 00:33:46.280
Can you tell that it is a red squirrel that's been feeding here?

00:33:45.600 --> 00:33:50.520
You can, because you can differentiate between the greys and the reds feeding.

00:33:49.760 --> 00:33:51.160
Greys are messier.

00:33:50.200 --> 00:33:55.080
They sort of get into it and throw it all over the place, where reds are a bit more delicate, like they are in their stature.

00:33:55.600 --> 00:33:58.640
Fair enough.

00:33:57.720 --> 00:33:59.080
Like their demeanour, general demeanour.

00:33:58.280 --> 00:33:59.800
Yes.

00:34:01.080 --> 00:34:06.280
You can sometimes see that when you come back to the trap if it's an absolute mess, you think, "Well, there's been a grey squirrel in there."

00:34:05.320 --> 00:34:10.560
The sweetcorn in here as well, sometimes you'll find that the reds don't actually eat that.

00:34:09.600 --> 00:34:16.239
They'll just leave a layer of sweetcorn, whereas the greys will plough through everything that's in there.

00:34:15.280 --> 00:34:16.560
Oh, OK.

00:34:15.800 --> 00:34:17.000
As well as looking at the feeder, you're also filming them.

00:34:16.040 --> 00:34:17.120
Yes.

00:34:18.880 --> 00:34:23.199
We've got this little camera down here.

00:34:22.440 --> 00:34:23.960
I presume it's going on this tree here.

00:34:23.360 --> 00:34:26.000
It's on this tree.

00:34:25.320 --> 00:34:28.000
I'll pass it round.

00:34:28.239 --> 00:34:29.360
If you pass it.

00:34:28.480 --> 00:34:29.719
That's lovely.

00:34:28.760 --> 00:34:29.840
That there.

00:34:28.920 --> 00:34:30.920
Are we a good height there, Ken?

00:34:31.080 --> 00:34:32.440
Yeah, I think that's going to be good.

00:34:31.840 --> 00:34:34.000
We'll get some good shots off that.

00:34:33.159 --> 00:34:34.560
Yeah, perfect.

00:34:33.960 --> 00:34:35.199
Good.

00:34:34.360 --> 00:34:36.520
Have you managed to film any yet?

00:34:35.560 --> 00:34:38.400
We've actually got some footage that I can show you, if you like.

00:34:37.440 --> 00:34:39.239
Yes, please!

00:34:38.400 --> 00:34:39.840
We'll go and have a look.

00:34:45.400 --> 00:34:47.719
So, I'll just load this one up for you, Matt.

00:34:46.920 --> 00:34:48.199
This is some footage we got...

00:34:47.239 --> 00:34:48.600
Oh, my word!

00:34:50.840 --> 00:34:52.000
Isn't that wonderful?!

00:34:51.040 --> 00:34:52.159
You can really tell what's been...

00:34:51.199 --> 00:34:53.239
Here he comes.

00:34:52.320 --> 00:34:53.639
Hello.

00:34:52.679 --> 00:34:56.040
Aren't they lovely-looking things, though?

00:34:55.199 --> 00:34:56.360
Beautiful.

00:34:55.679 --> 00:34:57.080
Absolutely beautiful.

00:34:56.199 --> 00:34:57.520
Obviously slightly wary at the moment.

00:35:00.640 --> 00:35:02.680
Yep.

00:35:01.880 --> 00:35:03.080
Oh, they are so dainty, aren't they?

00:35:02.120 --> 00:35:03.760
Beautiful.

00:35:03.120 --> 00:35:04.320
Look at him.

00:35:03.600 --> 00:35:05.160
Oh!

00:35:04.560 --> 00:35:06.280
I mean, real proof, obviously, that...

00:35:05.320 --> 00:35:07.240
That we've got them.

00:35:11.200 --> 00:35:16.040
That you've got them and that Squirrel Nutkin is alive and well here in the woods.

00:35:15.200 --> 00:35:18.200
Indeed.

00:35:17.320 --> 00:35:27.400
You should set some cameras up on the water's edge and see if you can get any of the squirrels rafting across to the island.

00:35:26.560 --> 00:35:29.080
Rafting across to the island, indeed, yeah.

00:35:29.320 --> 00:35:35.360
'Seeing evidence of real red squirrels in the woodland, 'it feels like a little bit of Beatrix Potter's magic 'has rubbed off on this place.

00:35:34.480 --> 00:35:38.000
'We'll leave them to gather their nuts in peace.'

00:35:38.360 --> 00:35:44.000
"Riddle me, riddle me, rot-tot-tote!

00:35:43.240 --> 00:35:48.800
"A little wee man, in a red red coat!"

00:35:48.520 --> 00:35:59.400
This year marks 300 years since the birth of the great landscape designer Capability Brown.

00:35:58.560 --> 00:36:13.120
Adam's in Suffolk finding out how a rare breed is being used to restore one of his famous landscapes.

00:36:21.280 --> 00:36:30.280
Capability Brown was responsible for changing the landscape of 18th-century England.

00:36:29.560 --> 00:36:35.880
He moved hills, created lakes and shaped the countryside.

00:36:34.920 --> 00:36:41.800
He worked on some of the most famous estates in the country, such as Blenheim Palace and Stowe.

00:36:43.680 --> 00:36:49.000
He was highly sought after by the aristocracy.

00:36:48.080 --> 00:36:54.520
It's thought that Brown worked on more than 170 gardens across Britain.

00:36:53.720 --> 00:37:00.000
I'm at one of them - Euston Hall in Suffolk - discovering one of his remarkable landscapes.

00:37:01.200 --> 00:37:07.440
To celebrate the anniversary, these grounds are being transformed to their former glory.

00:37:06.520 --> 00:37:16.840
What's really exciting is that the heavy work is being carried out by a magnificent team of Suffolk Punch horses, just as they would have done 300 years ago.

00:37:15.960 --> 00:37:18.720
I can't wait to see them in action.

00:37:19.040 --> 00:37:25.760
There's only one man in this country that has the horsepower to take on such a task.

00:37:27.080 --> 00:37:33.000
I met Nigel Oakley earlier this year.

00:37:32.040 --> 00:37:41.160
He breeds Suffolk Punch horses, and it's not often I meet anyone so passionate about a rare breed.

00:37:40.240 --> 00:37:43.200
Nigel is picking me up in style to see these beasts of burden in action.

00:37:42.320 --> 00:37:43.360
Hello, there.

00:37:43.080 --> 00:37:46.200
Hello, Nigel.

00:37:45.240 --> 00:37:46.720
How are you?

00:37:45.760 --> 00:37:46.800
Good to see you.

00:37:45.920 --> 00:37:48.640
Lovely to see you again.

00:37:47.800 --> 00:37:50.720
I should be calling you sir, shouldn't I?

00:37:50.520 --> 00:37:54.360
Something very similar, but not spelled quite that way.

00:37:55.120 --> 00:37:57.240
Yeah, please do.

00:37:59.600 --> 00:38:01.400
What a wonderful way to travel.

00:38:00.440 --> 00:38:04.240
Lovely, isn't it?

00:38:03.880 --> 00:38:12.040
To think we've got a Suffolk horse on Category 1 of the rare breeds pulling us along, it's a privilege for all of us.

00:38:13.360 --> 00:38:14.680
It really is.

00:38:13.800 --> 00:38:22.640
So the lords and ladies would have been taken around estates, in fact, this would have been their transport, wouldn't it?

00:38:23.920 --> 00:38:28.840
Well, it would have been the only form of transport available in that time.

00:38:27.960 --> 00:38:30.520
You know, you're talking the 1700s.

00:38:29.680 --> 00:38:33.040
That's 300 years ago.

00:38:32.080 --> 00:38:39.080
Horses were only really just coming into it, cos it had been oxen prior to that.

00:38:38.120 --> 00:38:39.240
In your mind, the Suffolk is one of the best.

00:38:38.320 --> 00:38:40.560
The Suffolk is indeed the best, yeah.

00:38:40.880 --> 00:38:48.000
What we're here for today, with the gardens, they would have been horsepower, the lakes would have been dug out by hand and the soil carted with horse and cart.

00:38:47.080 --> 00:38:48.320
There was no other way of doing it.

00:38:48.720 --> 00:38:55.400
You know, the JCB came a long while after.

00:38:54.640 --> 00:39:00.480
It's lovely seeing these Suffolks chain harrowing.

00:38:59.640 --> 00:39:01.960
It's a lovely sight.

00:39:01.080 --> 00:39:05.240
I mean, we could be looking at something 300 years ago.

00:39:04.640 --> 00:39:12.520
The chain harrows are pulling out the moss and levelling the ground, aren't they?

00:39:11.640 --> 00:39:12.920
Knocking down the molehills.

00:39:11.960 --> 00:39:13.240
Yeah, aerating the ground.

00:39:12.680 --> 00:39:19.600
Those horses, they're not just playing, they're actually doing a job.

00:39:20.120 --> 00:39:24.880
What do you think Capability Brown would have made of all of this, of what you're doing now?

00:39:24.040 --> 00:39:32.480
Well, hopefully, he'd have thought what he did 300 years ago is still in people's minds and they're still appreciating the work that he did initially.

00:39:31.800 --> 00:39:42.400
His nickname of Capability, apparently he went to somewhere like Euston Estates, looked at the land and said, "This land has great capabilities."

00:39:41.560 --> 00:39:52.120
Then, I think, in 1744, he married and ended up fathering nine children, so he was a man of great capability.

00:39:51.400 --> 00:40:05.320
To find out more about the restoration, I'm meeting with the Countess of Euston Hall, Lady Clare.

00:40:06.120 --> 00:40:13.720
30 years ago, Brown's original plans for the estate were uncovered, which means the grounds can now be renovated to his original design.

00:40:13.040 --> 00:40:18.080
How exciting was it when you found Capability Brown's drawing of your estate?

00:40:18.240 --> 00:40:21.880
It was so thrilling, because the whole thing had been lost.

00:40:20.920 --> 00:40:30.400
The river had silted up, there was nothing to be seen of these glorious lakes and broadwaters.

00:40:29.760 --> 00:40:33.760
Now you've brought it all back to life.

00:40:33.080 --> 00:40:37.480
Yes, in the last sort of two years, it's been totally opened up again.

00:40:36.720 --> 00:40:43.800
We had to move 60,000 tonnes of smelly mud from the river.

00:40:42.920 --> 00:40:44.160
60,000 tonnes.

00:40:43.400 --> 00:40:48.360
That must have meant, in the old days, 120,000 journeys.

00:40:47.680 --> 00:40:49.880
Quite incredible, isn't it?

00:40:49.080 --> 00:40:54.280
When you think of the scale of it, how many horses must have been working on the place.

00:40:53.320 --> 00:40:54.440
Just remarkable.

00:40:54.440 --> 00:41:03.840
Today you're celebrating the Suffolk Punch horse, but getting them to do some practical work in the boggy areas too.

00:41:03.400 --> 00:41:08.480
They couldn't be better suited for parkland work.

00:41:07.960 --> 00:41:10.280
They've got fairly small feet.

00:41:09.320 --> 00:41:10.760
Tractors make such a filthy mess.

00:41:10.440 --> 00:41:16.240
Everything had got so overgrown, we had to cut down all the old trees and pull them out.

00:41:15.440 --> 00:41:20.720
Horses are far better than tractors for that.

00:41:19.760 --> 00:41:27.760
I think they have got a great future in parkland restoration.

00:41:27.600 --> 00:41:31.040
These Suffolk Punches have such incredible power.

00:41:30.080 --> 00:41:39.480
They drag the logs to the edge of the woodland with ease, where they are then loaded on to a timber cart for transportation, using an ingenious method.

00:41:38.680 --> 00:41:42.560
How do you think we would load this log on here without the aid of any mechanical means?

00:41:41.720 --> 00:41:45.160
We've got to get it up onto this beam?

00:41:46.800 --> 00:41:49.800
Up onto here without Paul Daniels or anybody else.

00:41:48.960 --> 00:41:50.320
I don't know.

00:41:50.200 --> 00:41:51.480
I'm not sure.

00:41:50.800 --> 00:41:53.120
I don't know how you're going to lift it off the ground.

00:41:52.320 --> 00:41:54.240
Oh, I see.

00:41:55.360 --> 00:42:01.080
So they're using those logs as a bit of a ramp.

00:42:00.240 --> 00:42:01.600
Yep.

00:42:00.640 --> 00:42:02.880
Then the endless rope comes over to the whippletree.

00:42:02.080 --> 00:42:09.560
The whippletree's the spreader bar on the back of the horse which keeps the chains from his hocks.

00:42:08.720 --> 00:42:10.400
The endless rope will just twizzle it up.

00:42:12.000 --> 00:42:13.840
There we go.

00:42:13.120 --> 00:42:14.600
Wow, look at that.

00:42:13.720 --> 00:42:17.080
That's so clever, isn't it?

00:42:16.320 --> 00:42:17.680
Isn't it?

00:42:16.720 --> 00:42:24.440
Marvellous, really, when you consider a very, very simple technique and very little equipment to carry around with you.

00:42:24.200 --> 00:42:30.720
A rope doesn't weigh too much.

00:42:30.320 --> 00:42:32.320
So how many trunks would you get on here?

00:42:31.360 --> 00:42:40.480
Well, with a single horse pulling it and in these wet conditions where the ground's not that solid, probably five of those sort of diameter-length logs.

00:42:41.400 --> 00:42:45.480
Then you'd obviously take them to your depot, roll them off and then come back for another load.

00:42:48.200 --> 00:43:03.480
Incredible, the work of Capability Brown, but even more amazing, the man and the horsepower that created these beautiful views.

00:43:03.920 --> 00:43:05.600
Without the horses and the men, it could never have happened.

00:43:04.800 --> 00:43:06.080
We have a job to imagine it now.

00:43:05.160 --> 00:43:10.880
We've had, I don't know, 10 or 12 horses here today.

00:43:09.920 --> 00:43:15.560
This estate, even in the memory of Lady Clare, had 40 horses working here then.

00:43:14.880 --> 00:43:18.920
That was the intersection between horsepower and mechanisation.

00:43:18.200 --> 00:43:22.280
In the days of genuine horsepower, there must have been hundreds of them.

00:43:21.400 --> 00:43:23.640
Well, it's been a real treat to see them all coming together.

00:43:24.000 --> 00:43:26.120
Thank you for inviting me along.

00:43:25.160 --> 00:43:26.800
Not at all.

00:43:25.840 --> 00:43:27.280
Thank you.

00:43:26.400 --> 00:43:31.400
It's been a spectacle for me, although I work with them every day.

00:43:30.560 --> 00:43:32.480
Thanks very much.

00:43:38.960 --> 00:43:45.600
This may look like just a field of sheep, but later this week, it will be centre stage for one of the oldest sporting events in the country.

00:43:45.120 --> 00:43:52.360
This year marks the 130th anniversary of the Ambleside Show, and with it comes a showcase of traditional sports.

00:43:52.280 --> 00:43:58.920
For me, Lakeland sports show off this part of the world at its best.

00:43:58.040 --> 00:44:03.680
You get a whole host of dedicated, talented people taking each other on in a manner of different sports.

00:44:02.880 --> 00:44:06.840
The fell runners blow my mind.

00:44:07.480 --> 00:44:18.000
I used to sit on probably this very rock as a kid and watch them go up to the top, you'd see them trot along the horizon, and then they come thundering down at lightning speed.

00:44:17.200 --> 00:44:18.560
I don't even know how they stay on their feet.

00:44:17.800 --> 00:44:20.200
That's just one of the things that goes on.

00:44:20.360 --> 00:44:26.000
Cumberland wrestling, I mean, that is something else.

00:44:32.480 --> 00:44:35.640
Men have been wrestling here for centuries.

00:44:34.760 --> 00:44:42.400
It's a rough and ready sport which some believe came over with the Vikings.

00:44:44.200 --> 00:44:45.360
Wrestle.

00:44:44.520 --> 00:44:46.480
But now the girls are really getting stuck in.

00:44:45.720 --> 00:44:48.120
The first ever women's world championship for Cumberland wrestling is being held this year.

00:44:51.880 --> 00:44:57.360
20-year-old Connie Hodgson is going to show me the ropes.

00:44:56.920 --> 00:45:01.040
Now, there are many Cumbrian sights I'm proud of, and this is certainly one of them.

00:45:00.720 --> 00:45:04.000
Two sisters wrestling in a soggy field.

00:45:03.080 --> 00:45:06.520
Connie, let me pull you away for a second.

00:45:05.720 --> 00:45:09.320
I'll let you have a bit of a break, Hannah.

00:45:08.400 --> 00:45:11.640
How did you get into all this in the first place?

00:45:10.920 --> 00:45:16.320
Well, my dad's done it since he was a young lad, so we just started going to the academy with him and learnt how to do it.

00:45:15.760 --> 00:45:23.200
I'm pretty sure I've watched your dad wrestle over the years at Ambleside or Grasmere.

00:45:22.640 --> 00:45:26.880
I've seen a lot of this over the years, but I've never done it.

00:45:26.040 --> 00:45:27.920
Where do you start?

00:45:27.960 --> 00:45:29.440
Well, you start by shaking hands.

00:45:28.480 --> 00:45:30.720
Right.

00:45:29.840 --> 00:45:32.400
Then your left arm goes over and your right hand under.

00:45:32.880 --> 00:45:34.240
Then you hold like that.

00:45:36.160 --> 00:45:37.560
Once you get a hold, you're not allowed to let go.

00:45:36.720 --> 00:45:38.280
Right, OK.

00:45:37.320 --> 00:45:38.600
Oh, snuggly.

00:45:37.880 --> 00:45:39.640
Your hands need to be about...

00:45:42.880 --> 00:45:44.200
Yeah, there.

00:45:43.320 --> 00:45:45.480
Then your chin needs to be on the shoulder like that.

00:45:44.800 --> 00:45:46.680
It's quite intimate, isn't it?

00:45:45.720 --> 00:45:46.960
Yeah.

00:45:47.240 --> 00:45:51.080
The first move is called a back heel.

00:45:53.480 --> 00:45:54.760
You pull them in.

00:45:54.040 --> 00:45:55.640
Right.

00:45:55.840 --> 00:45:56.960
Then you put your heel behind theirs and lift it up and push them back.

00:45:56.120 --> 00:45:57.480
Yeah, right.

00:45:56.680 --> 00:45:58.160
Wrestle.

00:45:59.960 --> 00:46:02.400
That's it.

00:46:01.640 --> 00:46:03.200
Yay!

00:46:02.280 --> 00:46:03.760
Right, she's down.

00:46:02.800 --> 00:46:04.200
We're done.

00:46:03.280 --> 00:46:05.080
What's the hardest move?

00:46:07.640 --> 00:46:10.120
Eh, full buttock is quite hard.

00:46:09.160 --> 00:46:11.320
Full buttock?

00:46:10.360 --> 00:46:11.800
Yeah.

00:46:13.240 --> 00:46:19.320
So...you get a hold and then you go inside your opponent, like that, and you get them right behind you and stick your bum into them.

00:46:18.520 --> 00:46:19.840
Right.

00:46:18.960 --> 00:46:22.400
Put your leg across and pull them right round.

00:46:24.000 --> 00:46:27.640
I don't really know what happened there.

00:46:26.680 --> 00:46:27.920
Come on, be honest - how did I do?

00:46:27.040 --> 00:46:30.000
You did really well.

00:46:29.480 --> 00:46:31.280
You picked it up so well.

00:46:30.320 --> 00:46:33.840
Right, well, I really enjoyed that, but I think I'm done.

00:46:33.160 --> 00:46:34.440
I'll just peel myself off the floor.

00:46:33.920 --> 00:46:35.360
You know what?

00:46:34.400 --> 00:46:42.200
Women's wrestling might be in its infancy here, but there is one sport that's been challenging the locals for a lot longer.

00:46:45.360 --> 00:46:48.720
Connie, I'm reversing out the ring.

00:46:47.840 --> 00:46:54.120
Mark Addison is the current champion and has been running since he was eight years old.

00:46:53.160 --> 00:46:57.640
Here he is, our mountain goat.

00:46:56.920 --> 00:46:58.240
Good to see you, Mark.

00:46:57.560 --> 00:47:01.640
Right, talk me through your connection to the guide race.

00:47:00.840 --> 00:47:06.560
Well, as I was younger, I always used to come here and watch my dad run in this race.

00:47:05.680 --> 00:47:12.600
He was my hero, really, like most sons with their fathers, I always thought I wanted to do what Dad did, so...

00:47:12.640 --> 00:47:15.680
This isn't a sport that you can only do in your 30s and 40s, is it?

00:47:14.840 --> 00:47:16.040
No, no, it's...

00:47:15.240 --> 00:47:20.920
You get people still competing, 60s, even 70s, even 80s.

00:47:20.320 --> 00:47:22.480
It's pretty incredible.

00:47:21.520 --> 00:47:28.840
With the fell runners, obviously, people say we're a tough breed, so you get the older people coming through as well.

00:47:28.600 --> 00:47:30.560
Up here?

00:47:31.640 --> 00:47:33.040
Up here.

00:47:35.640 --> 00:47:49.120
The guide's race is thought to have started back in Victorian times when tourists seeking to take exercise used the knowledge and skills of local fellsmen to guide them over the mountainous landscapes.

00:47:48.320 --> 00:47:53.840
The guides were hugely competitive, and the race was born.

00:47:58.720 --> 00:48:01.840
It's quite steep, that, let's be honest.

00:48:01.600 --> 00:48:03.080
It is.

00:48:02.560 --> 00:48:05.520
That's the worst bit out the way, though.

00:48:04.560 --> 00:48:06.200
That's the steepest bit.

00:48:05.480 --> 00:48:07.840
This is about the halfway point now.

00:48:06.880 --> 00:48:09.440
Where are we going?

00:48:08.560 --> 00:48:12.440
So that rocky outcrop just out there, that's the top.

00:48:11.600 --> 00:48:14.960
That's where we're running up to, to turn to come back down.

00:48:14.080 --> 00:48:15.720
How long is this course?

00:48:14.920 --> 00:48:18.840
It's just under 2 miles, about 1.8 miles.

00:48:18.920 --> 00:48:20.800
So how typical is that for a fell race?

00:48:19.920 --> 00:48:26.040
Guide's races are typically short, steep, fast and intense.

00:48:25.160 --> 00:48:26.400
Have I imagined this or have I seen people up here cheering you on?

00:48:25.800 --> 00:48:27.000
Do you get a crowd up here?

00:48:26.320 --> 00:48:27.680
Yeah, you do.

00:48:27.080 --> 00:48:33.520
There's nothing better than when you come out of the ferns, you're puffing and blowing a bit and then they just give you a really big cheer, "Come on."

00:48:32.560 --> 00:48:36.720
That's either really motivational or really annoying.

00:48:37.040 --> 00:48:39.080
You're doing really well.

00:48:38.120 --> 00:48:39.600
I'm going to lead the way...

00:48:38.640 --> 00:48:40.240
Right, okey doke.

00:48:40.120 --> 00:48:42.080
..So I can set the pace.

00:48:47.280 --> 00:48:52.040
Climbing, not running.

00:48:51.440 --> 00:48:54.320
You're doing really well.

00:48:53.440 --> 00:48:54.840
Keep it going.

00:48:53.960 --> 00:48:56.600
I'm on my hands and knees.

00:48:56.680 --> 00:49:00.680
HELEN PANTS Nearly at the top now.

00:49:01.240 --> 00:49:02.560
Oh, my word!

00:49:01.720 --> 00:49:04.760
There we have it.

00:49:04.560 --> 00:49:12.800
This makes it all worth it.

00:49:13.480 --> 00:49:21.680
Right, well, admire it, take it in because, as they say in fell running, what goes up must come down.

00:49:20.920 --> 00:49:22.800
Looking forward to this bit, I think.

00:49:22.760 --> 00:49:23.960
Is he having a laugh?!

00:49:25.000 --> 00:49:28.480
You're just showing off now.

00:49:27.640 --> 00:49:32.840
I thought I was quite cool, now I feel like grandma.

00:49:43.880 --> 00:49:47.600
How do you stay on your feet?

00:49:46.800 --> 00:49:47.920
I don't know.

00:49:47.000 --> 00:49:48.280
Oh.

00:49:47.480 --> 00:49:49.000
'Ah, you don't.

00:49:49.840 --> 00:49:53.120
'Well, Mark did say, "What goes up must come down."

00:49:52.280 --> 00:50:00.160
'Having been a spectator of the Lakeland Games for years, 'I didn't appreciate just how tough they are until now.'

00:49:59.360 --> 00:50:00.600
The finish line is in sight.

00:49:59.720 --> 00:50:04.000
I can't keep control of my legs or my laughter.

00:50:03.200 --> 00:50:04.880
I don't know how you do that.

00:50:04.840 --> 00:50:07.480
You're all right with that, Helen.

00:50:06.760 --> 00:50:09.440
That was a proper good fell running fall, that.

00:50:08.560 --> 00:50:12.520
Good to see you bounce straight back up.

00:50:12.000 --> 00:50:13.320
I'll let you crack on without me.

00:50:12.520 --> 00:50:15.760
I think I've been a hindrance.

00:50:14.880 --> 00:50:17.480
Mark, absolute privilege.

00:50:16.520 --> 00:50:17.720
Thank you so much.

00:50:17.040 --> 00:50:18.520
My pleasure.

00:50:17.600 --> 00:50:18.760
Good luck.

00:50:17.800 --> 00:50:19.600
Catch you later.

00:50:18.840 --> 00:50:21.720
Don't tell anyone, but that really hurt.

00:50:29.480 --> 00:50:34.800
We're in Cumbria.

00:50:33.840 --> 00:50:38.600
And while Helen's been flat out learning Lakeland sports...

00:50:37.720 --> 00:50:41.400
I don't really know what happened there.

00:50:40.480 --> 00:50:43.200
And here we are.

00:50:42.440 --> 00:50:52.560
..I've been discovering the landscapes that gave rise to the fantastical tales of Beatrix Potter.

00:50:52.520 --> 00:51:00.800
But the landscape didn't just influence the author - the author also had a big impact on the landscape.

00:51:07.720 --> 00:51:14.640
She celebrated and championed rural life.

00:51:13.720 --> 00:51:24.240
She was determined to preserve this countryside that she loved so much, leaving many hill farms and acres of land to the National Trust.

00:51:23.640 --> 00:51:31.040
Despite being an outsider and a woman, she became president of the Herdwick Sheep Society.

00:51:30.120 --> 00:51:40.840
She founded a rural nursing trust, and she hosted camps for Girl Guides on her land at Hawkshead.

00:51:40.040 --> 00:51:48.040
Today, Hawkshead Guides and Brownies are working towards a new badge which celebrates 150 years since Beatrix Potter's birth.

00:51:49.080 --> 00:51:55.760
Now, to earn their badge, they have to complete three country tasks that she would have approved of.

00:51:54.880 --> 00:51:59.280
Now, obviously, I'm not a Girl Guide, so I'm not eligible for one.

00:51:58.320 --> 00:51:59.480
However, this lot are, so let's find out what's going on.

00:52:00.400 --> 00:52:01.960
Hello, everybody.

00:52:01.000 --> 00:52:02.160
Hello.

00:52:01.200 --> 00:52:03.840
Hiya, hi, hi.

00:52:02.920 --> 00:52:05.840
Who is going to tell me what's happening here?

00:52:04.920 --> 00:52:07.440
We've planted flowers.

00:52:06.640 --> 00:52:08.400
You've planted some flowers.

00:52:07.480 --> 00:52:09.240
Lovely.

00:52:08.320 --> 00:52:09.680
What have you been planting?

00:52:10.040 --> 00:52:11.160
Cos I can see a wonderful array of different plants.

00:52:10.240 --> 00:52:12.720
Does anybody know the different names?

00:52:12.080 --> 00:52:16.040
They're French marigolds.

00:52:15.320 --> 00:52:16.840
Ooh!

00:52:15.880 --> 00:52:17.280
Very good.

00:52:16.520 --> 00:52:20.760
Well, I mean, this all sounds like a wonderful idea, this Beatrix Potter badge.

00:52:19.880 --> 00:52:22.440
Where did the idea come from?

00:52:21.480 --> 00:52:25.960
Well, I'm a mad Beatrix Potter fan.

00:52:25.080 --> 00:52:28.440
'Julie Bell is the region chief commissioner.'

00:52:28.800 --> 00:52:33.240
She was a great conservationist, an environmentalist.

00:52:32.360 --> 00:52:35.840
I just think she was a really great woman.

00:52:34.960 --> 00:52:38.200
She's a really great role model for Girl Guiding.

00:52:38.000 --> 00:52:39.720
So, the girls have got a range of things to do.

00:52:38.880 --> 00:52:44.760
Some of that is around conservation and learning what's going on in their local area.

00:52:44.080 --> 00:52:45.960
Is that just happening in this area?

00:52:45.080 --> 00:52:51.840
It's in northwest England, we're the region that are promoting it, so I'm really proud about that.

00:52:50.960 --> 00:52:53.680
But, actually, anybody can do it in any part of the country.

00:52:52.960 --> 00:52:59.680
We've had our first e-mail from the States cos they would like to do it.

00:52:58.800 --> 00:53:06.360
The Potter badge challenges range from planting and growing food and flowers to building a wildlife habitat.

00:53:05.600 --> 00:53:11.080
This lot are definitely getting hands-on with nature.

00:53:10.480 --> 00:53:12.080
What have you found there?

00:53:14.760 --> 00:53:16.400
Rosy, Roxie, Kylie, Ross, Diane, Steven, and Erin.

00:53:15.640 --> 00:53:17.120
Wow.

00:53:16.280 --> 00:53:21.120
Which character do you think would have an absolute feast in here?

00:53:20.240 --> 00:53:23.160
Peter Rabbit.

00:53:22.360 --> 00:53:24.080
Peter Rabbit.

00:53:23.160 --> 00:53:28.080
I like the fact you've got Jemima Puddle-Duck running around as well.

00:53:27.400 --> 00:53:29.600
I think that's super.

00:53:29.520 --> 00:53:31.200
I've got more worms.

00:53:30.240 --> 00:53:31.320
Yeah, more worms.

00:53:30.720 --> 00:53:32.000
There's a lot of worms.

00:53:32.840 --> 00:53:35.400
That's what makes the soil so good.

00:53:34.440 --> 00:53:38.200
What's happening here?

00:53:40.000 --> 00:53:45.680
We're making, like, a bug hotel.

00:53:44.720 --> 00:53:46.160
Oh, my word.

00:53:45.440 --> 00:53:47.880
And we'll put the food on top of there.

00:53:48.040 --> 00:53:51.600
I would love to live in there.

00:53:50.680 --> 00:53:52.000
Is this all part of it, then?

00:53:51.240 --> 00:53:52.880
Yeah.

00:53:52.840 --> 00:53:53.920
That's how they get in.

00:53:52.960 --> 00:53:55.120
That's how they get in!

00:53:54.240 --> 00:53:58.280
Has that hotel got a spa?

00:53:57.320 --> 00:53:58.440
Cos if it has, check me in.

00:53:57.520 --> 00:54:00.240
Yes.

00:53:59.440 --> 00:54:02.640
I hear that you've certainly earnt your activity badge today.

00:54:01.680 --> 00:54:02.880
Yes.

00:54:01.960 --> 00:54:04.160
And you've earnt your Beatrix Potter badge.

00:54:03.240 --> 00:54:05.040
Well done.

00:54:05.480 --> 00:54:06.640
Well, there we are.

00:54:05.800 --> 00:54:08.240
You had a good day?

00:54:07.360 --> 00:54:08.880
Yes.

00:54:07.960 --> 00:54:09.280
Yeah.

00:54:08.600 --> 00:54:11.440
Yeah, this is all about the Beatrix Potter badge.

00:54:10.640 --> 00:54:12.640
Isn't that the most wonderful little sight?

00:54:11.760 --> 00:54:13.600
Gorgeous.

00:54:12.640 --> 00:54:17.920
If you were a little something, you could live in there until Jemima Puddle-Duck comes along and eats you.

00:54:17.680 --> 00:54:19.320
That's all we've got time for this week.

00:54:18.440 --> 00:54:23.200
Next week, we are going to be celebrating all things summer.

00:54:22.440 --> 00:54:23.840
I hope you're hungry.

00:54:27.040 --> 00:54:31.720
You're going to have to put some more on.

00:54:30.760 --> 00:54:33.960
I've done lots of harvesting in my time, but never like this before.

00:54:33.200 --> 00:54:35.280
Not in the river?

00:54:35.160 --> 00:54:37.000
No.

00:54:36.080 --> 00:54:37.840
This is absolutely spectacular, isn't it?

00:54:36.880 --> 00:54:38.120
Ooh!

00:54:37.240 --> 00:54:38.400
It's so close to us.

00:54:37.440 --> 00:54:38.720
That is literally heaven.

00:54:38.640 --> 00:54:39.720
But from all of us here, let's say goodbye on three.

00:54:38.760 --> 00:54:39.840
One, two, three...

00:54:38.960 --> 00:54:40.480
Goodbye!

00:54:39.520 --> 00:54:41.040
The heat in the Den is rising.

00:54:40.480 --> 00:54:41.880
You're coming across as, frankly, ridiculous.

00:54:41.000 --> 00:54:42.280
I'm flabbergasted by that.

00:54:41.400 --> 00:54:42.760
Will they get burnt...

00:54:41.800 --> 00:54:43.120
You have done an appalling job of selling them online.

00:54:42.240 --> 00:54:43.440
Erm...

00:54:42.600 --> 00:54:43.800
I'm...

00:54:42.840 --> 00:54:44.160
We're...

00:54:43.280 --> 00:54:44.480
We're...

00:54:43.600 --> 00:54:45.440
No, hang on.

00:54:44.480 --> 00:54:45.800
Sorry.

00:54:44.840 --> 00:54:46.160
I don't think I could work with you.

00:54:45.400 --> 00:54:46.600
Have you ever watched Dragons' Den?

00:54:45.640 --> 00:54:46.800
..Or will they set the world on fire?

00:55:52.720 --> 00:55:53.800
I like it.

00:55:53.560 --> 00:55:54.800
The scorching new series...

00:55:54.000 --> 00:55:56.840
It's OK to be excited.
